A||||5||DaveL||Loose Rocker Arm?||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||09-12-2001||07:43 AM||||What would be the symptoms of one or more loose rocker arms? Or maybe a partially collapsed lifter? 87 2.5||||reg||5
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-12-2001||08:07 AM||||Or some loose nut(s) that needs adjusted.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||09-12-2001||02:59 PM||||I'm almost sorry I asked. It didn't seem like a stupid question to me......||||reg||8
Z||000003||StuGood||09-12-2001||04:13 PM||||I think the answer to your original question is: Ticking sound; ticks proportionally faster when you increase engine speed, gets louder when you remove the oil filler cap from the valve cover.<P>I don't think there are any adjustment provisions for the lifters, on the 2.5-liter "Duke" motor.  Just on the 2.8-liter V6.   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-12-2001||07:22 PM||||4 cylinder rockers are not adjustable. You simply torque the bolt to about 24 pound feet.<P>87 motors are inherantly noisy in the vavles. You'll need to pull the valve cover and check things if you think something is bad.<P>Unless a lifter is totally flat, you probably won't be able to tell one is bad. You might be able to figure it out with a vacuum gauge but don't hold your breath.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||09-12-2001||09:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaveL:<BR><B>What would be the symptoms of one or more loose rocker arms? Or maybe a partially collapsed lifter? 87 2.5</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Dave!<BR>I had a ticking noise in my duke that went away as it warmed up...<BR>I thought it was valve train related, so I didn't check it out...<BR>Six months later, the piston dropped a skirt and tipped sideways...<BR>It was beautiful!!!<BR>Steam and oil out the back, but I did drive it home a mile away!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
